 ### Min-Max Hierarchy with Branch-on-Need Octree
-A technique to reduce the visited set of cells in marching cubes by embedding a min-max hierarchy in a branch-on-need octree to trivially ignore empty regions of a volume [S5, unofficial]. It uses the min-max hierarchy to identify empty regions of the volume [S5, unofficial]. This variant enables interactive rendering of data up to $512^3$ resolution [S5, unofficial].
+A technique to reduce the visited set of cells in marching cubes by embedding a min-max hierarchy in a branch-on-need octree to trivially ignore empty regions of a volume [S7, unofficial]. It enables interactive rendering of data up to $512^3$ resolution [S7, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The traversal time depends on the sparsity of the isosurface and the effectiveness of the pruning [S4, unofficial]. In the worst case, it is proportional to the number of nodes visited, which is bounded by the total number of nodes in the octree [S4, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** The method uses a single structure for both data and acceleration, reducing memory overhead compared to maintaining separate acceleration structures [S2]. The storage requirement is proportional to the number of octree nodes, with each internal node storing 16 scalar values (8 mins, 8 maxs) [S1][S4, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** Competitive frame rates are achieved on common multicore architectures, especially for large time-variant data [S2]. The method allows for interactive transfer function changes, which is a significant advantage over methods that require pre-computed classification tables [S1][S4, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Interactive transfer function changes; efficient memory usage; effective pruning of transparent regions [S1][S2][S4, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires the volume to be stored in an octree structure [S2].
-*   **When to use:** Suitable for large-scale volume rendering where interactive transfer function changes are required and the data can be represented as an octree [S1][S2].
-*   **When not to use:** Not given in the sources [S4, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ in the worst case where $N$ is the number of voxels, but typically much faster due to pruning. The complexity depends on the sparsity of the volume and the transfer function.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** The octree structure stores min/max values for each node. For a volume of size $V$, the memory overhead is proportional to the number of nodes in the octree. Compressed volumes can occupy 10-30% of the original size [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** Supports up to 90% reduction in sample computations for sparse data [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:**
+    *   Allows interactive changes to the transfer function without rebuilding the entire acceleration structure, as min/max ranges are stored and used to recompute majorants [S1][S6, unofficial].
+    *   Efficient memory order (cache hits) regardless of viewpoint [S1].
+    *   Suitable for large, time-variant data [S4].
+*   **Limitations:**
+    *   Requires preprocessing to build the octree and compute min/max values [S4].
+    *   At max depth, handling cap geometry adds complexity [S6, unofficial].
+    *   Less effective for highly dense volumes where most nodes contain the isovalue.
 
 ## Background
-The method traces rays through a min/max hierarchy inside interior octree nodes to accelerate traversal and avoid regions not containing the isovalue [S3]. It embeds the acceleration tree and scalar data in a single structure, employing optimized octree hash schemes to achieve competitive frame rates on common multicore architectures [S2]. This approach allows interactive transfer function changes by recomputing majorants from stored ranges, whereas direct computation of local majorants is not possible due to the indirection of the transfer function [S4, unofficial]. It has been applied to Adaptive Mesh Refinement (AMR) data rendering [S4, unofficial].
+The method was introduced by Knoll et al. in 2006 [S1][S4]. It was developed to address the difficulty of interactive rendering of large volumes, particularly where GPU memory limits and video bus bandwidth restrict real-time rendering of time-variant data [S4]. The technique embeds the acceleration tree and scalar data in a single structure, employing optimized octree hash schemes to achieve competitive frame rates on multicore architectures [S4]. It is a seminal implementation of min-max octrees for isosurface extraction and direct rendering [S3, unofficial].
 
